{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_37","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"caption\":\"Case v. Montana\",\"court\":\"Supreme Court of the United States\",\"date\":\"2026-01-14\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"607 U.S. 107; No. 24-624\",\"proposition\":\"The emergency-aid exception requires an objectively reasonable basis for believing that an occupant faces serious danger; officers need not establish probable cause before making the warrantless entry.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"probable_cause/emergency_aid_standard\",\"discovery_path\":\"SCOTUS 2026 Fourth Amendment standard search; https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/607/24-624/\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Chatrie v. United States\",\"court\":\"Supreme Court of the United States\",\"date\":\"2026-06-29\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 25-112\",\"proposition\":\"Government acquisition of Google Location History through a geofence warrant is a Fourth Amendment search. The Court remanded for assessment of probable cause and particularity at each stage of the warrant’s three-step disclosure process.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"probable_cause/geofence_warrants\",\"discovery_path\":\"SCOTUS geofence and probable-cause search; official slip opinion: https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/25-112_0am4.pdf\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Chiaverini v. City of Napoleon\",\"court\":\"Supreme Court of the United States\",\"date\":\"2024-06-20\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"602 U.S. 556; No. 23-50\",\"proposition\":\"Probable cause for one criminal charge does not categorically defeat a Fourth Amendment malicious-prosecution claim based on another charge that lacked probable cause; the inquiry is charge-specific.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"probable_cause/charge_specificity\",\"discovery_path\":\"SCOTUS 2024 probable-cause holdings search; https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/602/23-50/\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Noem v. Vasquez Perdomo\",\"court\":\"Supreme Court of the United States\",\"date\":\"2025-09-08\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 25A169\",\"proposition\":\"The Court stayed an injunction regulating immigration stops. Justice Kavanaugh’s concurrence, not a merits holding, stated that apparent ethnicity alone cannot create reasonable suspicion but may be considered with location, occupation, language, and other circumstances.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"reasonable_suspicion/ethnicity_and_immigration_enforcement\",\"discovery_path\":\"SCOTUS 2025 reasonable-suspicion and immigration-stop search; docket: https://www.supremecourt.gov/docket/docketfiles/html/public/25a169.html\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Chatrie\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-07-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"107 F.4th 319; No. 22-4489\",\"proposition\":\"The panel held that obtaining two hours of voluntarily enabled Google Location History was not a Fourth Amendment search and therefore did not require probable cause. The opinion was vacated upon rehearing en banc and its central search holding was later rejected by the Supreme Court.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"probable_cause/geofence_warrants_vacated_panel\",\"discovery_path\":\"Fourth Circuit geofence-warrant thread; subsequent rehearing order: https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/22-4489/22-4489-2024-11-01.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Chatrie (order granting rehearing en banc)\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-11-01\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 22-4489\",\"proposition\":\"The court granted rehearing en banc and vacated the panel opinion addressing whether geofence acquisition is a search and whether the warrant satisfied probable cause and particularity.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"probable_cause/geofence_warrants_en_banc_grant\",\"discovery_path\":\"Fourth Circuit en banc-grant search; https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/22-4489/22-4489-2024-11-01.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Chatrie (en banc)\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit en banc\",\"date\":\"2025-04-30\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"136 F.4th 100; No. 22-4489\",\"proposition\":\"An evenly divided en banc court produced no controlling answer on whether the geofence acquisition was a search, but affirmed denial of suppression through a one-sentence per curiam judgment; sufficient judges accepted Leon good faith. The Supreme Court later vacated the judgment.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"probable_cause/geofence_warrants_en_banc\",\"discovery_path\":\"Fourth Circuit en banc geofence search; https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/22-4489/22-4489-2025-04-30.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Smith\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-08-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"110 F.4th 817; No. 23-60321\",\"proposition\":\"A geofence warrant commanding a search of Google’s entire location-history database to identify unknown persons was a general warrant categorically prohibited by the Fourth Amendment. Suppression was nevertheless denied under Leon because officers reasonably relied on then-unsettled law.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"probable_cause/geofence_general_warrants\",\"discovery_path\":\"Geofence circuit-split search and Chatrie cross-citation; https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca5/23-60321/23-60321-2024-08-09.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Turner\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-12-04\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"122 F.4th 511; No. 22-4055\",\"proposition\":\"Arizona v. Gant’s authorization to search a recent arrestee’s vehicle when it is reasonable to believe evidence of the offense of arrest will be found imposes a standard less demanding than probable cause; the court declined to decide whether it exactly equals reasonable suspicion.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"standards_of_proof/gant_reasonable_to_believe\",\"discovery_path\":\"Fourth Circuit 2024 probable-cause standard search; https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/22-4055/22-4055-2024-12-04.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Jordan\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-05-01\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"100 F.4th 714; Nos. 23-3334 and 23-3347\",\"proposition\":\"Reasonable suspicion supported prolonging a traffic stop for a dog sniff when a quick-turnaround rental-car itinerary, inconsistent explanations, travel patterns, and other profile characteristics were assessed collectively. Courts may not neutralize the totality through a divide-and-conquer analysis merely because individual facts have innocent explanations.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"reasonable_suspicion/profiles_and_totality\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 circuit reasonable-suspicion and dog-sniff search; https://law.counselstack.com/opinion/united-states-v-terrence-jordan-ca6-2024\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Bejar-Guizar\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-07-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"142 F.4th 1188; No. 23-3201\",\"proposition\":\"In reviewing reasonable suspicion, the Ninth Circuit focused on whether the circumstances made criminal activity sufficiently likely, rather than on whether innocent behavior was likely. It upheld a Border Patrol stop based on the border-area location and contextual travel facts, creating or sharpening a claimed inter-circuit disagreement over how innocent explanations enter the totality analysis.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"reasonable_suspicion/innocent_explanations_split\",\"discovery_path\":\"Reasonable-suspicion circuit-split search; opinion and certiorari materials: https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/25/25-6640/391481/20260112203228616_2%20-%20Cert%20Petition.pdf\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Cortez\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the First Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-07-11\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"108 F.4th 1; No. 23-1029\",\"proposition\":\"A defense lawyer’s affidavit stating that counsel could not identify the defendant in surveillance footage did not make a substantial preliminary showing that the warrant affiant’s identification was deliberately or recklessly false. At the preliminary stage the court also considered the government’s explanation that identification rested on clothing, sharpening a claimed split over government rebuttal before a Franks hearing.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"franks/substantial_preliminary_showing_and_rebuttal\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 Franks hearing circuit-split search; opinion appendix and petition: https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/24/24-5757/327875/20241009160943353_Complete%20Petition%20and%20Appendix.FINAL.pdf\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. McGhee\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-04-11\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-1615\",\"proposition\":\"A court may request a government response while deciding whether the defendant has earned a Franks hearing, but it may not conduct a one-sided preliminary evidentiary proceeding that lets the government bolster the affidavit without giving the defense a meaningful opportunity to rebut the new evidence.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"franks/prehearing_government_rebuttal\",\"discovery_path\":\"Franks preliminary-hearing procedure search; https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca7/23-1615/23-1615-2024-04-11.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Osterman\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-10-23\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"110 F.4th 928; amended opinion\",\"proposition\":\"Although the affiant falsely described Wi-Fi evidence contradicted by CyberTip records in his possession, the court found negligence rather than reckless disregard. In testing materiality, it corrected the challenged statement and added the allegedly omitted exculpatory fact without adding unrelated omitted inculpatory facts, aligning the Seventh Circuit with one side of a claimed methodological split.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"franks/recklessness_and_corrected_affidavit\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 Franks corrected-affidavit split search; certiorari petition collecting the authorities: https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/24/24-851/341686/20250203111329351_Petition%20for%20Writ%20of%20Cert.pdf\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"Hughes v. Garcia\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-05-03\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"100 F.4th 611; No. 22-20621\",\"proposition\":\"Franks-based civil liability is not limited to the officer who signs the affidavit; an officer who directs or supplies intentionally or recklessly false material for a warrant may be liable. Removing eight adequately pleaded misstatements and omissions left the affidavit without probable cause, defeating qualified immunity at the pleading stage.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"franks/nonaffiant_liability_and_materiality\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 published Franks decisions search; https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca5/22-20621/22-20621-2024-05-03.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Norton\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Tenth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-03-05\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 24-2059\",\"proposition\":\"Franks applies to deliberately or recklessly false information supplied by an off-duty police officer who was actually involved in the investigation with the knowledge and acquiescence of on-duty officers; actual investigative involvement is a mixed question weighted toward factual review.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"franks/off_duty_officer_sources\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 federal-circuit Franks search; https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca10/24-2059/24-2059-2025-03-05.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Keller\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-12-09\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"123 F.4th 263; No. 23-40338\",\"proposition\":\"A trained and certified dog’s trained indication ordinarily establishes probable cause by itself. Distinct instinctive behavior that was not trained as an indication may contribute to probable cause only through the totality and supporting testimony; an unexplained weak response is not automatically sufficient. A dog’s untrained pawing at a vehicle was not itself a search.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"drug_dogs/trained_indication_vs_instinctive_alert\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 dog-alert probable-cause search; https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca5/23-40338/23-40338-2024-12-09.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Daniel\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-05-02\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-30491\",\"proposition\":\"A dog’s current annual certification supplied prima facie evidence of reliability under Florida v. Harris and shifted the burden to the defendant. Controlled testing deserves more weight than field statistics, and an 83.3% field success rate did not defeat probable cause.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"drug_dogs/certification_and_reliability\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 drug-dog reliability search; https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca5/23-30491/23-30491-2024-05-02.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Collier\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2024-09-06\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-3255\",\"proposition\":\"Absent contradictory circumstances, a trained dog’s alert supported probable cause where prior field accuracy exceeded fifty percent. A dog need not use one invariant final-response signal, and even a lower success rate may suffice when other facts support reliability.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"drug_dogs/field_accuracy_and_alert_form\",\"discovery_path\":\"2024 circuit drug-dog probable-cause search; https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca8/23-3255/23-3255-2024-09-06.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Garcia\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2026-03-05\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-4631\",\"proposition\":\"A certified narcotics dog’s recognizable but non-final alert supplied probable cause to search luggage even though the dog did not precisely identify the bag. The holding is limited by the dog’s training and credibility; evidence of cueing or an unbounded search could change the totality.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"drug_dogs/nonfinal_alert_and_scope\",\"discovery_path\":\"2026 published circuit dog-alert search; https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/23-4631/23-4631-2026-03-05.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Johnson\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fourth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2025-08-05\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"No. 23-4255\",\"proposition\":\"A narcotics-dog sniff at an apartment door from a common hallway was not a Fourth Amendment search under Katz because the sniff disclosed only contraband, and the hallway was not curtilage where the tenant lacked a right to exclude others. The decision expressly rejected the Seventh Circuit’s Whitaker approach and sharpened a circuit split.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"drug_dogs/apartment_door_sniff_split\",\"discovery_path\":\"2025 apartment-door dog-sniff circuit-split search; https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca4/23-4255/23-4255-2025-08-05.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Whitaker\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Seventh Circuit\",\"date\":\"2016-04-12\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"820 F.3d 849\",\"proposition\":\"A drug-dog sniff at an apartment door in a locked common hallway was a Fourth Amendment search because it used a detection device at the home and invaded the tenant’s heightened residential privacy. Johnson’s 2025 rejection of this analysis makes Whitaker newly central to the live circuit split.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"drug_dogs/apartment_door_sniff_search\",\"discovery_path\":\"Pre-2024 precedent identified through the Fourth Circuit’s 2025 Johnson split analysis; https://law.justia.com/cases/federal/appellate-courts/ca7/14-3290/14-3290-2016-04-12.html\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Scott\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2010-07-19\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"610 F.3d 1009\",\"proposition\":\"A dog sniff at an apartment door from a common corridor was not a Fourth Amendment search because it revealed only the presence of contraband. Recent decisions treat Scott as the principal pre-Jardines authority opposing Whitaker and supporting Johnson’s 2025 position.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"drug_dogs/apartment_door_sniff_no_search\",\"discovery_path\":\"Pre-2024 authority surfaced by recent apartment-sniff commentary and the Johnson/Whitaker split; recent federal-court discussion: https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCOURTS-mnd-0_23-cr-00372/pdf/USCOURTS-mnd-0_23-cr-00372-1.pdf\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}\n{\"caption\":\"United States v. Perez\",\"court\":\"U.S. Court of Appeals for the Eighth Circuit\",\"date\":\"2022-08-22\",\"cite_or_docket\":\"46 F.4th 691\",\"proposition\":\"The court avoided deciding whether Florida v. Jardines abrogated Scott’s apartment-door-sniff rule and instead applied good faith because officers relied on binding circuit precedent. The later Johnson/Whitaker split has made Perez’s unresolved treatment of Scott newly doctrine-shaping.\",\"doctrine_node\":\"drug_dogs/apartment_sniff_good_faith_after_jardines\",\"discovery_path\":\"Recent apartment-door dog-sniff commentary tracing Scott after Jardines; https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/USCOURTS-mnd-0_23-cr-00372/pdf/USCOURTS-mnd-0_23-cr-00372-1.pdf\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"}"}}
